0219 | \begin{proof}
0220 | Suppose that $a$ is a Zumkeller number. There are $a, k \in \mathbb{N}$ that $a=18k+r$ and $0 \leq r < 18$. If $0 \leq r \leq 12$, then it is clear that there is $r' \in \mathbb{N}$ that $a + r' = 18k + 12$. Hence, by Example \ref{firstexample}, it is a Zumkeller number. If $13 \leq r \leq 18$, then it is clear that there is a $r' \in \mathbb{N}$, $0 \leq r' \leq 12$ that $a+ r' = 18 (k+1)+6$. Therefore, by Example \ref{firstexample} it is a Zumkeller number.
0221 | \end{proof}
